WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.200
 So now that you've learned to program a microprocessor from the 1970s, I'd like to take a quick fast forward from the 1970s to the present.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:16.060
 Lots changed since the 6502 was available as a $25 microprocessor.

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:20.700
 Since the 1970s, there have been about 50 distinct CPU families.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.160
 I've been part of this my whole career, lots of research and innovation,

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:30.460
 adoption, switching, computer vendors picking one CPU and then switching to another CPU,

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:37.760
 generational turnover and overall approach from complex instruction sets to simpler risk instruction sets,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:47.500
 from large refrigerator size mainframes to small microprocessors, then larger microprocessors, faster microprocessors.

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.060
 Interestingly, after all that, things have settled down in the modern era.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:59.560
 There are two major CPU families in use if you think about consumer and server and desktop.

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:09.900
 If you take computers and phones and bring them together, about 90% of the marketplace is ARM-based processors and the remaining 10% is Intel processors.

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.560
 ARM is very successful because it was focused on low power.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:20.260
 And as computers have gotten faster and faster, we really care more and more about saving power,

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:27.220
 both for small handheld devices and for desktops and then even laptops.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.260
 And so power savings is really important and ARM was designed to save power.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:37.900
 So let's take a quick case study and look at Apple over the years.

00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:45.460
 Apple, the Apple I was one of the early adopters of the 6502 in 1997.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:54.360
 And the Apple II and the Apple II and its sort of whole family were a very successful set of microprocessors for a long time.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:02:01.560
 Apple switched to a different processor, the Motorola 68000 from 1984 to 1994.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:12.620
 During that time, they were playing with a project called the Newton, which was a handheld device, wasn't extremely popular, but it was a handheld device.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:18.560
 Battery powered handheld sort of pocket, but that was using an ARM processor, an ARM processor.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:23.560
 Then they switched from the Motorola to the PowerPC in 1994.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:35.660
 And again, 12 more years they use the PowerPC and then the Intel x86 in 2006, they switched their Macintosh line from using PowerPC to Intel x86.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:41.720
 I was actually in the audience when Steve Jobs announced the switch from PowerPC to Intel x86.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:49.700
 I was amazed, I was sitting, I was working on my laptop, which had a PowerPC in it, had been using it for years.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:57.620
 And at the end, he threw the doors open and we ran into some rooms and there were some prototype Intel x86 Macintoshes running Mac OS.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:04.020
 And it was amazing to me that they could switch from PowerPC to Intel x86.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:12.120
 Then the iPhone came out in 2007 and I was also in the audience when Steve Jobs announced the first iPhone, this iPhone 1.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.920
 And I was carrying around at that point in time, I was carrying around a trio as my main computer.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:28.820
 And the trio was one of the things he put up on the screen when he was talking about the iPhone, but the iPhone had an ARM, a Samsung ARM in it.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:39.520
 And so this is an ARM processor. And then in 2020, the ARM came not just to the iPhone, but from the Macintosh.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:46.220
 ARM is not manufactured by one organization. ARM is licensed to many folks to build them.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.820
 And Apple builds its own hardware in the Macintosh from 2020 to present.

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:55.820
 And what's interesting about that is Apple is currently redesigning their CPUs continuously.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:02.720
 Every year they have a new release. They call it the M1, the M2, the M2 Max, M2 3 Pro or whatever.

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.320
 And what they're doing is they're actually studying what needs to be in their microprocessor.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:15.820
 And they add instructions to their ARM systems based on what they think we use these for,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:21.420
 whether it's image processing or audio or video gaming or now increasingly artificial intelligence.

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:28.420
 And one of Apple's advantages is because they use the ARM and they licensed the core instructions to the ARM,

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.920
 but then they add their own instructions and then manufacture them.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:38.420
 Another thing about Apple that's kind of interesting is you've been using an emulator,

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:44.720
 a code that's written in JavaScript that makes it look like you can run 6502 processors.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:53.120
 Well, in 2005, as Apple was moving from PowerPC to Intel processors, they included a technology called Rosetta 1.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:00.020
 And then when they were moving from Intel to ARM processors in 2020, they included a technology they called Rosetta 2.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:06.120
 And of course, that's a reference to the Rosetta Stone, a famous stone with the connected multiple languages

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.820
 from sort of antiquity to Latin based languages.

00:05:10.820 --> 00:05:19.020
 And so what Rosetta did in both cases, it translated the machine code from a previous computer to the modern one.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:25.020
 And the interesting thing was, is it would often run because the modern processors they were choosing,

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.020
 whether it be the Intel initially or ARM later,

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:36.620
 they were so fast that they could emulate the previous generation processors almost at the same speed as the hardware of the previous generation.

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:43.520
 And so this really made it a lot easier for those of us who were Apple customers making a transition from architectures,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:56.120
 which otherwise would have almost been impossible to go from one major CPU architecture to another architecture.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:04.820
 So fun aside, there is sort of this indirect connection between the 6502 and the ARM.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:15.620
 And ARM stands for Acorn Risk Microprocessor, and it was built in Cambridge, England by Acorn.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:25.020
 And Acorn originally built a thing called the BBC Micro, which was a 6502 based microcomputer, more popular in the UK.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:32.420
 And so they decided they were going to create a new microprocessor, so they knew a lot about 6502.

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:40.520
 The goal was not to make a better 6502, but there was a lot of homage to the 6502 in the ARM.

00:06:40.520 --> 00:06:44.720
 And so they used BBC Micros to build and simulate the ARM design.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:53.120
 ARM 1 had 25,000 transistors, the 6502 had 4,500 transistors, and the ARM 1 through whatever,

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:58.820
 there's many generations of ARMs, and they are the most widely used processors in the world.

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:07.120
 And for a good time, you can ask AI to make this historical leap connection between the 6502 ARM and the Raspberry Pi.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:15.920
 And I have a picture of Sophie Wilson, one of the architecture designers of the initial ARM working at Acorn,

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:20.320
 working on both the BBC Micro and the building of the ARM microprocessor.

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:30.880
 you

